From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: seedds <seedds@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 00:58:28 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Event descriptors are numbered as the bsd service numbers
 them, the lowest free one from 0, and Select is Poll over its sets:
 Minecraft's NetherNet put its descriptor (0x4000) in a 1024-bit fd_set on the
 stack and crashed 2 KB past it (PR #1651)

The host numbered its event descriptors from 0x4000. Games size their tables for
the service's small numbers: Minecraft's NetherNet threads (Bedrock 1.21.120) put
theirs in an fd_set on the stack for Select, and FD_SET at main+0x18b2ae8 read and
wrote 2 KB past the set (SIGBUS in "NetherNet Network" right after start). Select
also failed as soon as a descriptor was set, so it could never wait on one.

- The descriptors are the lowest free number from 0 (0-63).
- Select (both overloads) turns the descriptors in its read and write sets into
  pollfds for dm_sk_poll and returns the sets of the ready ones; one the host never
  made has nothing to say, as in Poll; one closed during the wait fails with EBADF.
  Only numbers below EFD_MAX are passed on, so the array stays small on the guest
  thread's stack.
- As a game's stale or never-valid descriptors now fall in the same range, Poll
  reports POLLNVAL only for a descriptor that was open when the wait began.

t_net_eventfd covers the numbering, reuse, Select's sets, a never-made descriptor
inside the range, and a close during Select. Ocarina of Time (gRPC's wakeup
descriptor) boots past grpc_init on a host pinned to its libraries' chain.

Co-authored-by: Aoba_d1snet <18624035+Haseenesss@users.noreply.github.com>

Upstream-Status: backport eddae74f
Reason: Minecraft's NetherNet puts an event descriptor in an fd_set; upstream fixed it after the pin (seedds/nxnative#1651), the next sync drops this
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#769
